Found by the k69 review (2026-11-21): when the MCP server cannot listen (e.g. "address already in use"), the launcher keeps running. With k69 an editor give-up then logs "the launcher keeps serving MCP" although no server is running — the message is false and an external agent has nothing to connect to.

Done when: a failed MCP listen ends the launcher with exit 1 and a clear error before the editor or the agent start (or, if that is too strong, the editor handler receives the real serving state) — with a test using an occupied loopback port. Lane: unreagent-worker. Touches cmd/launcher/main.go run() — after k69/k70/k71 lands.

2026-11-21 08:41 Decision (main agent; worker carried it out): a failed MCP listen ends the launcher with exit 1 and a clear error before the editor, the bridges or the agent start. Worker's own decisions on top: (1) the bind (listenMCP, cmd/launcher/main.go) runs right after config load and the CLI flags, before the window-mode log rotation, the stale-script sweep, skill materialization and the .mcp.json writes, so a second unreagent on the same project leaves the running one's files alone. (2) An error from Serve after a successful bind (anything but ErrServerClosed) is not silent either: serveMCP logs 'MCP server on <addr> stopped serving: <err> — stopping everything.' and ends the session as a failure (exit 1). Why: the agent's MCP tools and its permission prompts fail from then on, and an external agent has nothing to connect to, so running on without MCP would be the silent failure the bind check is there to prevent. Only a permanent Accept error gets there; http.Server retries temporary ones itself. (3) The k69 give-up line 'the launcher keeps serving MCP' is now reached only while the server serves: MCP enabled at that point means bound and serving, a Serve failure ends the session, and the supervisor's stop path fires no OnExit. makeEditorExitHandler did not need to change.

